{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_011CfY2HKFQSWrdAfyVPVew9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017aXVPWPdCbyWJLxu52bGaR","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"[REDACTED:local-path:6e63cc43278a]/mnt/outputs/artifacts/deck-review-acmecorp/handoff/20260929T160143Z/checklist_output.json","content":"{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "purpose_clear",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 1 states 'Acmecorp — AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders', which names the category (developer tools) and ICP (indie SaaS founders) but omits a quantified outcome. Sequoia's 'Writing a Business Plan' framework requires '[Category] for [ICP] that delivers [quantified outcome].' The outcome ('saves 20+ hours/week') appears on the problem slide but is never consolidated into the purpose statement on slide 1.",
      "notes": "Rewrite the title slide tagline as a single declarative outcome sentence: e.g., 'Acmecorp — the CLI that saves indie SaaS founders 20+ hours a week on tooling integration.' Pull the quantified benefit from the problem slide into the purpose statement so an investor can repeat it after reading slide 1."
    },
    {
      "id": "headlines_carry_story",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 2's content ('Indie SaaS founders waste 20+ hours/week on developer tooling integration') functions as a conclusion. Slides 3, 5, and 6, however, state facts rather than conclusions: 'Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI' names the feature; '2 co-founders, ex-Stripe (eng) and ex-Notion (product)' is a label; 'Raising $1.2M seed at $8M post' is a data point without framing. DocSend and Sequoia recommend every headline be a conclusion an investor can skim and retain.",
      "notes": "Rewrite the solution, team, and ask headlines as conclusions: e.g., 'One command replaces 20+ hours of integration work' (solution), 'Operator-engineer duo with direct experience building the tools we replace' (team), 'Raising $1.2M to reach $50K MRR and Series A readiness' (ask)."
    },
    {
      "id": "narrative_arc_present",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The deck follows Problem → Solution → Traction → Team → Ask, covering the core arc. However, it is missing Why Now, Market Size, Business Model, GTM, Competition, and Financials — six of the nine slides in the seed-stage expected framework. The arc feels incomplete: after the traction slide, investors have no context for market opportunity, competitive landscape, or how the funds will be used. Sequoia, DocSend, and YC all require the full arc for a seed deck.",
      "notes": "Add the missing slides to complete the narrative arc: Why Now (what changed to make this the right moment), Market Size (bottom-up), Competition (honest with differentiators), Business Model (pricing and margins), GTM (channel and early proof), and Financials (burn, runway, operating model)."
    },
    {
      "id": "strongest_proof_early",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "Traction is slide 4: '$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth (last 4 months).' DocSend data shows 82% of readers who reach slide 4 finish the deck. The strongest proof point — 18% MoM growth and paying customers — appears within the first 4 slides, meeting the criterion threshold."
    },
    {
      "id": "story_stands_alone",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The deck's individual slides are readable in isolation, but key connections are absent. Slide 3 ('Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI') does not explain how this solves the 20+ hours/week problem on slide 2. Slide 6 ('Raising $1.2M seed at $8M post') provides no context for what the money buys or what milestones it funds. DocSend's share-deck standard requires a deck that is self-explanatory without verbal accompaniment.",
      "notes": "Add one sentence to the solution slide connecting the product to the problem: e.g., 'Each integration that used to take hours is now a single command.' Add a milestone plan to the ask slide so the round makes sense without a founder presenting it in person."
    },
    {
      "id": "problem_quantified",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 2 quantifies the pain with a specific time metric ('20+ hours/week') and names the ICP ('indie SaaS founders'). However, no source or customer evidence is provided for the figure, and no cost of inaction is stated. Sequoia's framework requires the user, the moment of pain, and the quantified cost — the ICP and metric are present, but the evidence basis and stakes are absent.",
      "notes": "Source the '20+ hours/week' claim with customer discovery data or a direct customer quote. Add a cost-of-inaction statement: e.g., 'For a 2-person team, that is 40+ hours of engineering time lost per month — time not spent on the product.'"
    },
    {
      "id": "solution_shows_workflow",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 3 states: 'Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI.' This is a feature description, not a workflow demonstration. There is no before/after flow, no product screenshot, no demo link, and no stated value delivered. YC's seed deck guide explicitly states: focus on value delivered, not technical architecture. DocSend's standard requires at least one of: product screenshot, 3-step workflow, or demo link — even at seed stage.",
      "notes": "Add a 3-step before/after workflow to the solution slide: show the time each integration used to take, then show the single command that replaces it. Include a product screenshot or terminal output to prove the product exists and make the time savings visible."
    },
    {
      "id": "why_now_has_catalyst",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no 'why now' slide. Sequoia's framework asks 'Why wasn't this built before?' — this question is unanswered. The proliferation of SaaS building blocks (GitHub, Stripe, Vercel, Linear APIs), the growth of indie SaaS founders, and the rise of AI coding tools that accelerate product development but leave tooling integration unsolved are plausible catalysts that are never stated.",
      "notes": "Add a 'why now' slide naming the specific catalyst: the simultaneous availability of GitHub, Stripe, Vercel, and Linear APIs, the post-COVID explosion of indie SaaS founders, and the gap created by AI coding tools that speed up product work but do nothing about tooling integration overhead."
    },
    {
      "id": "market_bottom_up",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no market size slide. Sequoia and YC require bottom-up sizing — ICP count × willingness to pay × adoption rate. Without a market sizing slide, investors cannot assess whether Acmecorp is venture-scale. A generic top-down TAM chart is not present either.",
      "notes": "Add a market sizing slide using a bottom-up approach: count the indie SaaS founder ICP (e.g., Indie Hackers, Product Hunt communities), apply a realistic ARPU (the current implied $333/month per customer is an anchor), and model a reachable adoption rate to arrive at a serviceable addressable market."
    },
    {
      "id": "competition_honest",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no competition slide. DocSend data shows investors spend 88% more time on the competition section in funded decks vs. unfunded ones. Omitting this slide leaves investors unable to assess whether the founders understand their competitive landscape or have a defensible position.",
      "notes": "Add a competition slide mapping direct competitors (existing CLI tools, platform-native integrations), incumbents (custom scripts, Zapier/Make for less technical founders), and the 'do nothing / build internally' alternative. Use 2-3 concrete differentiators — speed, breadth of integrations, developer-native UX — rather than a generic 2x2 matrix."
    },
    {
      "id": "business_model_clear",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no business model slide. The traction slide implies approximately $333 average revenue per customer ($8K MRR / 24 customers) but provides no pricing structure, buyer vs. user distinction, or gross margin information. Sequoia's framework requires pricing model, buyer vs. user distinction, and gross margin structure — none of these are addressed.",
      "notes": "Add a business model slide showing: pricing tier(s) (seat-based, usage-based, or tiered), who pays vs. who uses (the indie founder is both), and a rough gross margin estimate. Clarify whether pricing is per-seat or per-project and how ARPU scales."
    },
    {
      "id": "gtm_has_proof",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no GTM slide. The traction slide shows 24 paying customers but provides no information on how they were acquired, the sales cycle, ACV expectations, or channel strategy. Sequoia and DocSend require ICP + channel + early proof (pipeline, conversion, CAC, payback) at seed stage.",
      "notes": "Add a GTM slide explaining how the first 24 customers were acquired (community outreach, direct sales, PLG) and what the repeatable motion looks like at scale. State the primary channel (e.g., developer communities like Indie Hackers, Hacker News, Product Hunt) and any early conversion or CAC data."
    },
    {
      "id": "team_has_depth",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 5 states: '2 co-founders, ex-Stripe (eng) and ex-Notion (product).' No names, LinkedIn profiles, photos, or specific accomplishments are included. DocSend data shows the team slide receives approximately 46 seconds of attention — about 30% of total investor reading time. Sequoia and YC require specific evidence of what founders built, not just where they worked.",
      "notes": "Add full names and LinkedIn URLs. Replace employer names with specific accomplishments: e.g., 'Built Stripe’s webhook delivery system' or 'Led product for Notion’s API from 0 to 50K developer integrations.' Add a founder-market-fit statement explaining why this team is uniquely positioned to build Acmecorp."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_structure",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The seed-stage expected framework calls for: Title, Problem, Solution, Why Now, Market Size, Traction, Business Model, Team, Ask — nine slides. The deck has six slides covering Title, Problem, Solution, Traction, Team, and Ask. Missing: Why Now, Market Size, Business Model, Competition, GTM, and Financials. The structure is substantially incomplete for a seed-stage deck per all major frameworks (Sequoia, YC, DocSend).",
      "notes": "Add the missing seed-stage slides: Why Now, Market Size, Business Model, Competition, GTM, and a simple Financials slide. Each missing slide represents an investor question that will be asked in a first meeting and is better answered proactively in the deck."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_traction",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 4 shows '$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth (last 4 months)' — MRR and growth rate are appropriate seed-stage metrics. However, the seed-stage framework requires a retention signal (churn or cohort data) alongside revenue metrics, which is absent. DocSend and a16z flag MRR without retention as an incomplete traction picture at seed stage.",
      "notes": "Add a retention signal to the traction slide: monthly churn rate, a 60-day cohort retention figure, or net revenue retention. Even a single data point ('customers active after 60 days: X%') substantiates the quality of the MRR and addresses the question investors will ask first."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_financials",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no financials slide. The seed-stage framework (a16z, Carta) requires at minimum: operating model, revenue assumptions, gross margin, burn rate, and runway. The ask slide mentions $1.2M but provides no runway calculation, burn rate, or gross margin information.",
      "notes": "Add a financials slide with: current monthly burn, a rough gross margin estimate, and an 18-24 month operating model showing the revenue trajectory and when the raise is exhausted. State explicitly what the company looks like at the end of the runway."
    },
    {
      "id": "ask_ties_to_milestones",
