    {
      "id": "purpose_clear",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 1 states 'Acmecorp — AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders', which names the category (developer tools) and ICP (indie SaaS founders) but omits a quantified outcome. Sequoia's 'Writing a Business Plan' framework requires '[Category] for [ICP] that delivers [quantified outcome].' The outcome ('saves 20+ hours/week') appears on the problem slide but is never consolidated into the purpose statement on slide 1.",
      "notes": "Rewrite the title slide tagline as a single declarative outcome sentence: e.g., 'Acmecorp — the CLI that saves indie SaaS founders 20+ hours a week on tooling integration.' Pull the quantified benefit from the problem slide into the purpose statement so an investor can repeat it after reading slide 1."
    },
    {
      "id": "headlines_carry_story",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 2's content ('Indie SaaS founders waste 20+ hours/week on developer tooling integration') functions as a conclusion. Slides 3, 5, and 6, however, state facts rather than conclusions: 'Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI' names the feature; '2 co-founders, ex-Stripe (eng) and ex-Notion (product)' is a label; 'Raising $1.2M seed at $8M post' is a data point without framing. DocSend and Sequoia recommend every headline be a conclusion an investor can skim and retain.",
      "notes": "Rewrite the solution, team, and ask headlines as conclusions: e.g., 'One command replaces 20+ hours of integration work' (solution), 'Operator-engineer duo with direct experience building the tools we replace' (team), 'Raising $1.2M to reach $50K MRR and Series A readiness' (ask)."
    },
    {
      "id": "narrative_arc_present",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The deck follows Problem → Solution → Traction → Team → Ask, covering the core arc. However, it is missing Why Now, Market Size, Business Model, GTM, Competition, and Financials — six of the nine slides in the seed-stage expected framework. The arc feels incomplete: after the traction slide, investors have no context for market opportunity, competitive landscape, or how the funds will be used. Sequoia, DocSend, and YC all require the full arc for a seed deck.",
      "notes": "Add the missing slides to complete the narrative arc: Why Now (what changed to make this the right moment), Market Size (bottom-up), Competition (honest with differentiators), Business Model (pricing and margins), GTM (channel and early proof), and Financials (burn, runway, operating model)."
    },
    {
      "id": "strongest_proof_early",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "Traction is slide 4: '$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth (last 4 months).' DocSend data shows 82% of readers who reach slide 4 finish the deck. The strongest proof point — 18% MoM growth and paying customers — appears within the first 4 slides, meeting the criterion threshold."
    },
    {
      "id": "story_stands_alone",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The deck's individual slides are readable in isolation, but key connections are absent. Slide 3 ('Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI') does not explain how this solves the 20+ hours/week problem on slide 2. Slide 6 ('Raising $1.2M seed at $8M post') provides no context for what the money buys or what milestones it funds. DocSend's share-deck standard requires a deck that is self-explanatory without verbal accompaniment.",
      "notes": "Add one sentence to the solution slide connecting the product to the problem: e.g., 'Each integration that used to take hours is now a single command.' Add a milestone plan to the ask slide so the round makes sense without a founder presenting it in person."
    },
    {
      "id": "problem_quantified",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 2 quantifies the pain with a specific time metric ('20+ hours/week') and names the ICP ('indie SaaS founders'). However, no source or customer evidence is provided for the figure, and no cost of inaction is stated. Sequoia's framework requires the user, the moment of pain, and the quantified cost — the ICP and metric are present, but the evidence basis and stakes are absent.",
      "notes": "Source the '20+ hours/week' claim with customer discovery data or a direct customer quote. Add a cost-of-inaction statement: e.g., 'For a 2-person team, that is 40+ hours of engineering time lost per month — time not spent on the product.'"
    },
    {
      "id": "solution_shows_workflow",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 3 states: 'Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI.' This is a feature description, not a workflow demonstration. There is no before/after flow, no product screenshot, no demo link, and no stated value delivered. YC's seed deck guide explicitly states: focus on value delivered, not technical architecture. DocSend's standard requires at least one of: product screenshot, 3-step workflow, or demo link — even at seed stage.",
      "notes": "Add a 3-step before/after workflow to the solution slide: show the time each integration used to take, then show the single command that replaces it. Include a product screenshot or terminal output to prove the product exists and make the time savings visible."
    },
    {
